WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.175
Now the crossing situation, Rule fifteen, and it’s the one you’ll use every single day.

2
00:00:06.425 --> 00:00:10.750
Two power-driven vessels are crossing, with a risk of collision.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.175
Which one keeps out of the way?

4
00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:17.175
The vessel that has the other on her own starboard side.

5
00:00:17.425 --> 00:00:19.250
Look at the diagram.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.700
The lower boat sees the other boat off to her right.

7
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.200
So she’s the give-way vessel.

8
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:32.350
And the rule adds one more thing: if the circumstances allow, she avoids crossing ahead of the other vessel.

9
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:38.350
So her best move is usually a clear turn to starboard, passing behind the other boat.

10
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.300
Here’s the memory trick, and it works at night.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.675
If you’re looking at the other boat’s red sidelight, red means stop.

12
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:48.900
You’re the give-way vessel.

13
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.775
If you’re looking at her green sidelight, green means go.

14
00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:56.875
You’re the stand-on vessel, and you hold your course and speed.

15
00:00:57.125 --> 00:00:58.850
Red, give way.

16
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.925
Green, stand on.

17
00:01:01.175 --> 00:01:06.425
Just remember that it only works for two power-driven vessels in sight of each other.
